<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Static color painted by the system compositor, via android:windowBackground
    in themes.xml, for the tiny gap before this window's real Compose content
    (the theme's gradient background) has drawn its first frame - see the
    windowBackground comment in themes.xml for the full explanation, including
    a correction there about an earlier version of this fix that used the
    wrong attribute (windowBackgroundFallback) and never actually painted
    during this gap.

    THE FIX for "app still shows a white/Ocean-blue flash on first launch even
    though the real default is meant to be Slate": this file previously used
    Ocean's own "#F2F8FF" sky-white gradient stop, chosen back when
    OceanPalette was genuinely the app's default theme (see the version
    history in this file's own comment before this edit). Ocean is no longer
    the default - a fresh install with no theme chosen yet now resolves
    through AUTO_THEME_ID -> resolveThemeId() to ProfessionalPalette
    ("Slate") on a light-mode device (see Theme.kt) - but this static XML
    value was never updated to match, so the very first painted frame still
    showed Ocean's blue-white regardless of which theme the person would
    actually land on a moment later once Compose's real first frame drew.
    Changed to "#F4F5F7", ProfessionalPalette's own solidBackground (see
    DialerColors.kt), so the placeholder now blends into Slate's first frame
    the same way this file's original Ocean value blended into Ocean's - same
    principle as before, just pointed at the theme that is now actually
    default. Both MainActivity and the in-call Activity use this same
    drawable.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <solid android:color="#F4F5F7" />
</shape>
